<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Pierre Boyer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Pierre Boyer was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from 1818 to 1843. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>'s death in 1820 he reunited the north with the republic, and in 1822 he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, ruling all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> until 1844. In 1825 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s demand for an indemnity of 150 million francs in return for recognition, a lasting burden. He was overthrown in 1843 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer.jpg" alt="Jean-Pierre Boyer" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B.C. (Name unknown, beyond these initials) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Jean-Pierre_Boyer_of_Haiti_(Hispaniola_Unification_Regime)_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boyer was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> on 15 February 1776, the biracial son of a French tailor and an African mother, a former slave from the Congo. His father sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for his education. In the early years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> he fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, and he allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">André Rigaud</a> in Rigaud's unsuccessful insurrection against Toussaint in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. After the War of the South he escaped to France with Rigaud and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a>. On 3 August 1800 the French privateer Vengeance, on which he sailed, was captured by the USS Trumbull, and he was held at New London, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, where local Freemasons, as he was himself, treated him well.</p><p>He returned in 1802 with the French troops of General Charles Leclerc. When it became clear that the French meant to reimpose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and restrict the free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gens-de-couleur-libres">gens de couleur</a>, he joined the patriots under Pétion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>. In November 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew its surviving 7,000 troops, less than a third of those sent, most having died of yellow fever.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Pétion died in 1818 and Boyer succeeded him as second President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, an arranged transition, since Pétion had chosen him and the Senate approved. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>'s soldiers rebelled in 1820, Christophe, in failing health and fearing assassination, killed himself, and Boyer reunited Haiti without a single battle.</p><p>On 30 November 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a> declared the independence of the Spanish side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. Boyer feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> might use it as a foothold, and in February 1822 he annexed it with a force of 50,000 soldiers, entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on 9 February.</p><p>In 1824 nearly 6,000 Americans, mostly free blacks, migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, and most returned within a short time because the administration could not support them. Boyer believed independence required recognition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On 11 July 1825 he signed a treaty by which France would recognise Haiti in return for 150 million francs within five years, a sum reduced to 90 million in 1838. He had to borrow 30 million francs from France for the first instalment. The Rural Act of his government tied the rural population to its smallholdings and set production quotas, and people resisted being tied to other people's land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Boyer's rule ended in 1843, when the poor economic situation was made worse by the 1842 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a> earthquake and the rural majority rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-riviere-herard">Charles Rivière-Hérard</a>. On 13 February 1843 Boyer fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, then settled in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 9 July 1850. He had governed for just under 25 years, the longest rule of any Haitian leader.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Pierre Boyer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dessalines, Emperor Jacques I, had 3,000 to 5,000 remaining whites killed and was assassinated in 1806; Henri Christophe's northern kingdom and Alexandre Pétion's southern republic were reunited by <b>Jean-Pierre Boyer</b> in 1820, who conquered Santo Domingo in 1822.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Pierre Boyer</b> tried to reverse the trend with the Rural Code of 1826, which bound labourers to plantations and restricted their movement, but it proved largely unenforceable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1825 Charles X recognised Haitian independence only for 150 million francs, ten times the price of Louisiana, delivered under the guns of fourteen brigs of war; <b>Jean-Pierre Boyer</b> signed on 11 July 1825, and the debt drained Haiti's treasury for a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Pierre Boyer</b> then reunited the north with the Republic of Haiti.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Pierre Boyer</b>, president of the Republic of Haiti, marched east with an army, and Santo Domingo surrendered without resistance on 9 February 1822.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-spanish-haiti">Republic of Spanish Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.55,-72.34&amp;year=1824" aria-label="Port-au-Prince on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2591 1476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean-Pierre Boyer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3041 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3041 1746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.55,-72.34&amp;year=1824">What was here in 1824? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
